{
  "args": "mcp --repo [tmp]/store --as agent/worker-17",
  "steps": [
    {
      "request": {
        "id": 1,
        "jsonrpc": "2.0",
        "method": "initialize",
        "params": {
          "capabilities": {},
          "clientInfo": {
            "name": "swarmops-tests",
            "version": "0"
          },
          "protocolVersion": "2025-11-25"
        }
      },
      "response": {
        "id": 1,
        "jsonrpc": "2.0",
        "result": {
          "capabilities": {
            "tools": {}
          },
          "content": null,
          "instructions": "SwarmOps is source control for coding-agent swarms: many agents commit to one store at once. Each tool is one store operation, and its result is the SwarmOps envelope {ok, op, api, result | error, meta}, as structuredContent and again as JSON text. A failed operation sets isError and gives error {code, message, retryable, exit, details, hint}: retryable says whether the operation can succeed when it is run again, as it is or after re-reading what the hint names, and a hint is often a swarmops command, whose operation is the tool of the same name (ref list is ref_list, cat read_file, ls read_tree, info repo_info). commit records a changeset; with advance it also moves that ref to it in the same transaction, if the ref still names parent: check result.advanced. ref_set and ref_delete change a ref by compare-and-swap, so their expected_old, like commit's parent with advance, is the full 64-hex changeset id the ref held when you read it (from ref_list, show, log or a commit's result). A ref name or a relative revision is read when the call runs instead of pinning the id you read: the ref's own name always matches, so the compare-and-swap protects nothing, and main~1 on ref main never matches; an id prefix stops resolving once another changeset shares it; the server refuses all three. To publish a changeset, land it: land merges it three-way onto main and moves main in one step, and on E_CONFLICT details.report says what to resolve. A read may show a commit that a failed sync then takes back; a mutation that names it fails under the lock instead of building on it. op_log lists the store's transactions, newest first; undo restores the refs one moved while they still hold what it set; main moves only forward: land a revert. Clients limit long tool results: ask for little, as each tool's description says. A {\"file\": path} content is read by this server, not by your client: give an absolute path (a relative one, or the server's own stdin or stdout, is refused). Every change is recorded for the agent the server runs as.",
          "protocolVersion": "2025-11-25",
          "serverInfo": {
            "name": "swarmops",
            "title": "SwarmOps",
            "version": "[version]"
          }
        }
      }
    },
